WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.850 --> 00:00:06.750
1,577 job listings arrived in Andrew's inbox in three days.

2
00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.250
He didn't read them.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.500
Something else did —

4
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:12.850
with better manners and a much longer attention span.

5
00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.050
It works from the alert emails he already gets —

6
00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.550
no scraping, no bot accounts.

7
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:23.550
Twice a day, eight specialist agents read every alert and rank what survives.

8
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:26.500
The hard rules are plain code, not AI.

9
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.650
Rules where rules suffice; models where judgment is needed.

10
00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:35.900
Aggregators rename titles, inflate pay, and advertise jobs that are already gone.

11
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.950
Every survivor gets researched back to the employer's real posting.

12
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:44.400
Three days in: 40 closed roles caught, 14 ghosts,

13
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.000
and pay estimates off by 20% or more — 41 times.

14
00:00:49.150 --> 00:00:51.500
What's left lands on one board:

15
00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:57.650
26 roles, each with a fit score built from evidence quoted out of the posting and Andrew's resume.

16
00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.300
Not a vibe — a citation.

17
00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.250
And the part Andrew cares about:

18
00:01:02.250 --> 00:01:06.400
every claim in a tailored resume is checked against the resume he actually has.

19
00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:10.100
An unsupported line holds the package — not his reputation.

20
00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:12.100
The system never submits on its own;

21
00:01:12.100 --> 00:01:14.750
a human names every application.

22
00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.750
Three days. Zero sent. Working as designed.

23
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.550
Twice a day, the machines read everything and decide nothing.

24
00:01:22.550 --> 00:01:25.100
Andrew reads almost nothing and decides everything.
